   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to permitting\nthe Culinary Institute of America to both produce and sell alcoholic beverages\nand permits certain donations",
    "basePrintNo": "S8989",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8989-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §101, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8989\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to\npermitting the Culinary Institute of America to both produce and sell\nalcoholic beverages and permits certain donations\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would allow the Culinary Institute of America in Hyde Park, NY\nto carry out its academic mission without certain encumbrances (commonly\nreferred to as Tied House Laws) established in the Alcoholic Beverage\nControl Law.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nWith respect to the Culinary Institute of America:\n\n§ 1 - Amends section 101 of the alcoholic beverage control law that\nallows alcohol wholesalers and manufacturers to have interest in an\nalcohol retail license\n\n§ 2 - Amends section 101 of the alcoholic beverage control law that\nallows licensed manufacturers and wholesalers to do business with\nlicensed retailers\n\n§ 3 - Amends section 101 of the alcoholic beverage control law that\nallows the Culinary Institute to engage in activities that are essential\nto a student's training and a faculty member's advancement, including\nbut not limited to consulting and partnering with alcohol beverage\nmanufacturers and wholesalers and accepting sponsored scholarship oppor-\ntunities\n\n§ 4 - Allows alcoholic beverage retailers to do business with manufac-\nturers and wholesalers.\n\n§ 5 - Effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation will allow the Culinary Institute of America (CIA) to\ncarry out its academic and pedagogical mission without being encumbered\nby the Prohibition-era Tied House laws that were put in place to prevent\nthe alcohol and beverage industry from being controlled by a few domi-\nnant alcohol manufacturers and wholesalers.  These laws prohibit liquor\nretailers from having an interest in a business that manufactures or\nwholesales alcoholic beverage. However, they were not designed with the\nneeds of higher education institutions that focus on the culinary arts\nin mind, nor should we assume that the intent of these laws was to stunt\nthe academic offerings and approaches of the Culinary Institute. The CIA\nholds several liquor licenses for their on-campus restaurants that serve\nas experiential learning environments for students. Because the CIA\nholds retail licenses, the Tied House prohibitions could effectively\npreclude it from building relationships with its natural corporate part-\nners: beverage manufacturers and wholesalers. Moreover, to further its\nacademic mission and to meet the workforce demands of the fast growing\nhospitality industry, the Culinary Institute would like the option of\nworking with industry experts to train its students in the distilling of\nalcoholic beverages. The current Tied House prohibitions could render\nthat academic advancement impossible.\n\nThe CIA is amongst the most renowned academic institutions of its kind\nin the world. To maintain that status, the Culinary Institute must\ncontinue to expand its academic offerings to meet market demand. As with\ncolleges and universities of other disciplines, it must also foster\nindustry partnerships that not only help offset the cost of tuition\n(through scholarships, endowed professorships, and funding for campus\nbuildings) but also expose students to the leading practitioners in the\nculinary and beverage industry. Just as an engineering university part-\nners routinely with employers like IBM or Google to train tomorrow's\nengineers, the Culinary Institute needs assurance that it can partner\nwith the beverage industry and can offer its students a broad learning\nexperience that includes elements that are currently prohibited under\nthe Tied House laws.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPACT ON THE STATE:\n\nNo cost for state or local government. Additionally, by giving the Culi-\nnary Institute the flexibility it needs to educate culinary arts\nstudents, this bill would contribute to the expansion of hospitality\njobs in New York State and the attendant tax revenues.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill. However, it is modeled after similar exemptions that have been\nafforded in statute to other beverage-related institutions, including\nthe New York State Wine and Culinary Center, the Concord Grape Belt\nHeritage Association, and Finger Lakes Wine Center.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nEffective immediately.\n",
