    "lawCode": "Amd §101, rpld §101 sub 7, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8989A\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to\nauthorizing the manufacture of beer, spirits, cider, wine and mead at\nthe Culinary Institute of America; and to repeal certain provisions of\nsuch law relating thereto\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would allow the Culinary Institute of America (\"CIA\"), a\nworld-renowned, higher education institution in Hyde Park, New York to\ncarry out its academic mission by allowing for its exemption from the\n\"Tied House Laws\" established in the New York State Alcoholic Beverage\nControl Law. The \"Tied House Laws\" prohibit manufacturers and whole-\nsalers of alcoholic beverages from having any interest, direct or indi-\nrect in any premise where any alcoholic beverage is sold at retail.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\n§ 1 - Amends paragraph a of section 101 of the Alcoholic Beverage\nControl Law by adding a new subparagraph (xiv), to allow any such prem-\nises or business constituting an alcoholic beverage manufacturer or\nwholesaler in the Town of Hyde Park, County of Dutchess owned and oper-\nated by the CIA, a not-for-profit 501 (c)(3) higher education institu-\ntion chartered by the New York State Board of Regents operating within\ncertain meets and bounds, to be exempt from the prohibition that alcohol\nwholesalers and manufacturers may not have any interest, direct or indi-\nrect in any premise where any alcoholic beverage is sold at retail.\n\n§ 2 - Adds a new paragraph (f) to subdivision one of section 101 of the\nAlcoholic Beverage Control Law to exempt the CIA from the prohibitions\nand restrictions contained in paragraphs (b), (c) and (d) to allow for\ncontractual and other financial arrangements undertaken by the Culinary\nInstitute of America for the education purposes of such institute,\nincluding student scholarships, academic building sponsorships, and\nInstitute event sponsorships that further the academic mission of the\nCulinary Institute of America, where such contractual or other financial\narrangements are between a licensed manufacturer, wholesaler, or retail-\ner for on-premise consumption, and the Culinary Institute of America,\noperating within the metes and bounds established by subparagraph (xiv)\nof this subdivision.\n\n§ 3 - Repeals subdivision seven of section 101 of the Alcoholic Beverage\nControl Law.\n\n§ 4 - Effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation will allow the Culinary Institute of America (\"CIA\") to\ncarry out its academic and pedagogical mission without being encumbered\nby the Prohibition Era \"Tied House Laws\" that were put in place to\nprevent the alcohol and beverage industry from being controlled by a few\ndominant alcohol manufacturers and wholesalers.  These laws prohibit\nliquor retailers from having an interest in a business that manufactures\nor wholesales alcoholic beverages. The CIA holds several liquor licenses\nfor their on-campus restaurants that serve as experiential learning\nenvironments for students. Because the CIA holds retail licenses, the\n\"Tied House\" prohibitions could effectively preclude it from building\nrelationships with its natural corporate partners: alcoholic beverage\nmanufacturers and wholesalers.  Moreover, to further its academic\nmission and to meet the workforce demands of the fast-growing hospital-\nity industry, the CIA would like the option of working with industry\nexperts to train its students in the distilling of alcoholic beverages.\nThe current \"Tied House\" prohibitions essentially render that academic\nadvancement impossible.\n\nThe CIA is amongst the most renowned academic institutions of its kind\nin the world. To maintain that status, they must continue to expand\ntheir academic offerings to meet market demand. As with colleges and\nuniversities of other disciplines, it must also foster industry partner-\nships that not only help offset the cost of tuition (through scholar-\nships, endowed professorships, funding for campus buildings, etc.) but\nalso expose students to the leading practitioners in the culinary and\nbeverage industries. Just as an engineering university partners routine-\nly with employers like IBM or Google to train tomorrow's engineers, the\nCIA needs assurance that it can partner with the beverage industry and\ncan offer its students a broad learning experience that includes\nelements that are currently prohibited under the \"Tied House Laws.\"\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill. However, similar exemptions have been afforded in statute to\nother alcoholic beverage-related institutions, including the New York\nState Wine and Culinary Center, the Concord Grape Belt Heritage Associ-\nation, and the Finger Lakes Wine Center.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNo cost for state or local government. By giving the Culinary Institute\nof America the flexibility it needs to educate culinary arts students,\nthe bill will contribute to the expansion of hospitality jobs in New\nYork State and the attendant tax revenues.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
